Isoniazid-Induced Severe Hepatotoxicity (DILI) secondary to Anti-Tubercular Therapy (ATT)

1. Case Scenario and Patient Narrative

### Initial Information
* Report Type: Initial Spontaneous Report
* Country of Origin: India
* Primary Source: Healthcare Professional (Pulmonologist / Hepatologist)

### Patient Demographics
* Patient Initials: R.K.
* Age: 48 years
* Gender: Male
* Weight: 62 kg
* Relevant Medical History: Pulmonary Tuberculosis (newly diagnosed). Non-smoker, no history of alcohol consumption, baseline liver function tests were normal prior to treatment initiation.

### Suspected and Concomitant Drugs
* Suspected Drug: Isoniazid (INH) 300 mg orally once daily (Indication: Pulmonary Tuberculosis; Duration: Initiated 6 weeks prior to admission).
* Concomitant Anti-TB Regimen: Rifampicin 600 mg, Pyrazinamide 1500 mg, and Ethambutol 1000 mg orally once daily (Fixed-Dose Combination regimen).

### Case Narrative and Timeline
* Week 1: Patient initiated on standard 4-drug Anti-Tubercular Therapy (ATT) in India following a positive sputum smear for acid-fast bacilli.
* Week 6 (Day 1): Patient presented to an outpatient clinic with a 5-day history of progressive jaundice, dark tea-colored urine, right upper quadrant abdominal discomfort, severe fatigue, loss of appetite, and persistent nausea.
* Week 6 (Day 1 Diagnostic Findings):
  * Total Bilirubin: 8.4 mg/dL (Reference: 0.2 to 1.2 mg/dL; Direct: 5.6 mg/dL).
  * Alanine Aminotransferase (ALT): 1,120 U/L (Reference: 7 to 56 U/L; >20x Upper Limit of Normal).
  * Aspartate Aminotransferase (AST): 980 U/L (Reference: 10 to 40 U/L; >24x Upper Limit of Normal).
  * Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP): 180 U/L (Mild elevation; primary hepatocellular pattern).
  * Viral Hepatitis Serology (A, B, C, E): Negative.
* Action Taken: All hepatotoxic anti-tubercular agents (Isoniazid, Rifampicin, Pyrazinamide) were immediately discontinued. Patient was admitted to the hospital and managed with supportive care and non-hepatotoxic background regimen (Ethambutol + Levofloxacin).
* Outcome: Over the course of 3 weeks post-discontinuation, jaundice completely resolved, serum total bilirubin dropped to 1.1 mg/dL, and ALT/AST levels normalized to baseline levels.

